Driving in high heels is dangerous!
A number of misjudged pedal accidents among young people (Auto Messe Web) - Yahoo! News
Violating in violation of the Road Traffic Act
There are more and more reports of accidents caused by mis-operation, such as pedal missteps, are attracting attention, but it is a big mistake to think that it is only a problem for elderly drivers.
In the number of pedal misguided accidents by chronology (Police Agency data), the highest number of pedal missteps was 1080cases under the age of twenty-four, exceeding 1032cases for those over the age of seventy-five.
It’s never enough for the elderly, and it’s important for young people to pay the same attention.
Are you going to pick up your feet?
Are you going to be safe?
This data does not describe the gender-to-female-to-female-to-female-to-to-female-to-contigation, but unlike the elderly who are often thought to come due to the decline of the body, such as driving skills and reflexes, young-aged people may make inadvertent mistakes due to distracted by other things such as music and conversation, as well as missteps due to their clothes.
In particular, some women choose high heels, pumps, sandals, mules, and various shoes according to the clothes of the day and drive it as it is.
There are some people who know that it is not suitable for driving, but there are many people who do not think about the possibility of becoming dangerous at all and are driving without malice.
However, driving with high heels and sandals violates road traffic laws.
The first is to “ensure the operation of the steering wheel, brakes, and other operations” and “violation of safe driving obligations” stipulate that “you must not drive in a way that causes an accident and cause harm to a person.”
For example, the Miyazaki Prefecture Road and Traffic Law Enforcement Law stipulates that the driver must comply with the matters established by each prefectural public safety committee. For example, in the Miyazaki Prefectural Road and Traffic Law Enforcement Regulations, Article XII states, "Do not drive a vehicle (excluding light vehicles) by wearing footwear that may interfere with driving operation or wearing footwear that are likely to interfere with driving operation."
In each prefecture, even if it is not specified as a high heel, it is the same purpose that driving with "a footwear that may interfere with driving operation" is regarded as a violation.
So why is it dangerous to drive in high heels?
This is obvious when you consider the basics of pedaling safely.
In order for safe and reliable pedal operation, the heel of the foot is first fixed to the floor, the foot should be perpendicular to the brake pedal, and when you step on the accelerator pedal, tilt the foot to the right with it as a fulcrum.
At this time, regardless of the behavior of the car, it is important to fix the heel firmly so that it does not shake.
However, in high heels, the area where the heel is grounded on the floor is small, and it always shakes, and because there is an angle to the entire foot at the height of the heel, the area of the foot grounded to the pedal is also small, making it difficult to make a fine adjustment.
I think this can lead to danger.
In addition, there is a floor carpet in most cars, but in this material, the heel of the high heel is easy to catch, and the heel is caught and the pedal does not step at the case of a sudden brake, or the high heel is taken off at the time when the heel is caught, the high heel may be taken off, and it may not be able to step on the back of the pedestal.
In addition, there is a case that the heel got stuck to the back side of the pedal when stepping on the pedal and then turning it back, and the accelerator pedal did not come back.
Accelerating joint development of new AWD vehicles and 86/BRZ next-generation models (response) - Yahoo! News
On September 27th, Toyota Motor Corporation and SUBARU agreed to a new business capital tie-up.
Toyota acquires an additional share of Subaru and converts it into an equity-method-appropriate affiliate.
The two companies have been working to collaborate on contract production of Toyota vehicles by SUBARU in a business alliance in 2005, as well as the joint development of the FR sports car "86" and "BRZ".
In June of this year, the company also agreed to work on the development of an EV-only platform and an EV-only platform using Toyota's electrified AWD (full-wheel drive) technology and Toyota's electrification technology.
In the business capital tie-up agreed this time, Toyota will acquire additional Subaru shares.
At present, Toyota's share of Subaru's equities has a voting rights ratio of 16.83%, but it will be acquired additionally until it reaches 20%.
Subaru will become an equity-method affiliate of Toyota.
On the other hand, SUBARU will acquire Toyota's shares, which are equivalent to the same amount (maximum of eight billion yen), which is equivalent to the amount required for Toyota to repurchase Subaru shares.
The two companies will develop and strengthen their cooperative relationship through a business-capital alliance.
We will promote the joint development of AWD models and 86/BRZ next-generation models, the expansion of THS (Toyota Hybrid System) in Subaru vehicles, cooperation in the connected area, and technical cooperation in the autonomous driving field.
