Growing sakura prawns in the process PT, 290m deep in water, such as in Shizuoka City|SHIZUOKA SHIMBUN AT-S
sakura prawns born this year who were successfully photographed in the deep waters of Suruga Bay = 11th, off Kamahara, Shimizu-ku, Shizuoka (provided by "Yui Sakura Shrimp Project")
The Yui sakura prawn project of a marine industry cluster council led by industry, academia and government in Shizuoka City and other organizations has recently succeeded in photographing sakura prawns, which are thought to have been born during the spawning season of this spring and summer on the 290-meter-deep seabed in Suruga Bay.
The image of the growth process is valuable, and the people involved want to lead to the elucidation of the ecology of succulent prawns, which have many mysteries.
The photo was taken near the bottom of the seabed about one and a half kilometres in the Kamabaru area of Shimizu-ku, Tokyo.
I checked about three hundred in seventeen minutes.
The conventional knowledge that sakura prawns in the deep-sea are "forming a certain herd on the seabed during the day" has been re-backed.
The adult body was about 40mm in length, and the individual that was reflected was about 23mm in body length.
We identified the spawning season by comparing it with the seasonal data of sakura prawns landed.
In-depth analysis will be provided by Fujitsu Software Technologies and Shizuoka College of Industrial Science and Technology, which make up the team.
Keisuke Mizuno, the project leader, says, "We want to capture the living image of individuals in the growth process, including young shrimps."
