Renewed mobile phone prices Price reduction in fair competition: Hokkaido Shimbun Doshin electronic version
A new pricing plan for mobile phone companies has been introduced in accordance with the enactment of the revised Telecommunications Business Act in October.
However, it remained largely elevated.
Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshihide Suga said last summer that there was "room room to lower the price by about 40 per cent," and even though there were high expectations for price cuts, there was no price competition.
Rakuten's full-scale entry has been postponed, and the oligopoly status of the three major companies is a major factor.
For consumers, it is desirable to have a fee system that is easy to use due to fair competition.
I’m looking forward to the efforts of mobile phone companies.
The revised law prohibits a set discount on the terminal fee and communication fee.
This is because the communication fee was set to be relatively expensive instead of selling the terminal fee at a low price.
SOFTBANK and KDDI (au) have announced plans to reduce their devices by up to halve.
However, both companies have "SIM locks" that can't be used by third-party lines for a hundred days.
NTT Docomo will reduce the cost of terminals by a maximum of one-third for subscribers of its own lines.
It seems that all three companies are continuing "enclosures" to prevent them from transferring to other companies.
With regard to SIM locks, the Ministry of Internal Affairs and Communications (MIC) has announced a policy to require immediate release.
The companies also need to deal with it urgently.
Rakuten's postponement is because the development of telecommunications networks such as base stations did not proceed as planned.
Since it was expected to be a detonator of price-reducing competition, it is a slap in the face.
Was there a glimmer of hope?
It is also not clear the timing of the start of the full-scale service and the price.
As a company in charge of social infrastructure, I would like you to take responsibility for your business.
This price revision was triggered by Chief Cabinet Secretary Suga's remarks.
Suga expressed his dissatisfaction about the new fees of the three companies, saying, "I think that the competition will start from now on. I do not think that it will be good as it is at all."
However, I don’t think that “government-made price reductions” will distort the market and encourage healthy competition.
The company should develop customer-oriented competition before it is interfered with by the government.
Each company's fee structure is very complex and easy to compare.
The Consumer Affairs Agency cautioned that there are cases in which the burden on consumers is more than halved, even though the device is "up to halved".
In order not to cause misunderstandings of users, the companies themselves must strive for polite and easy-to-understand explanations.
