In conjunction with the United Nations Climate Action Summit held in New York, the city of Vancouver held a number of various events to think about global warming since the end of last week.
On the 27th of the last day, there was a school strike called "Fridays for the Future".
The students who took a break from the school on this day appealed to the adults to "protect the earth even if they take time off from the important thing of the school", and many adults who took time off from work answered, "We will do our best for the future of the children who are seriously studying and thinking."
Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries (Gifu Shimbun Web) - Yahoo! News
The Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries on the 27th announced a draft amendment to the epidemic prevention guidelines that will allow preventative vaccinating domestic pigs, which are not currently permitted, in order to prevent the spread of the spread of the livestock infectious disease "Ton cholera".
The policy is to select nine prefectures, including Gifu and Aichi Prefectures, which have been confirmed to be infected with pigs and wild boars.
Vaccinated live pigs and fertilized eggs will be restricted to within the region, but the distribution of butchers and processed products outside the region will be permitted.
We aim to complete the procedure as soon as possible by the end of October through public comments to the general public (opinion solicitation).
By using the vaccine and allowing the distribution of meat outside the region, it is no longer a "clean country" certified by an international organization, and it may affect the export of pork.
Since the outbreak in September last year, the status of a clean country in Japan has been temporarily on hold.
In the revised plan, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries set up a "precommended vaccination area" based on the opinions of experts on areas where there is a high risk of transmission from wild boars to pigs.
Eligible prefectures have created a "vaccination program" that summarizes the scope and timing of vaccinations, the number of vaccinations, how to proceed, etc.
The other seven prefectures that have been listed as recommended for vaccination are Saitama, Toyama, Ishikawa, Fukui, Nagano, Mie, and Shiga.
When the guidelines are revised, the region will consider the situation and make a new decision.
As a general rule, the movement of inoculated pigs, semen, fertilized eggs, carcasses, exfoliators, etc. shall be restricted within the region in principle.
In view of the costs associated with restrictions on distribution and its impact on farmers, it will not be subject to meat and processed products, and will be allowed to be circulated virtually outside the region.
In order not to spread the spread of the virus, we will thoroughly dispose of food waste.
He advocates for the safety of inoculated pigs and works to prevent reputational damage.
The proposed revision of the guidelines was compiled at a meeting of experts held on the same day, and was confirmed at a meeting of the anti-epidemic response headquarters.
In the future, we will carry out opinion inquiries and public comments to prefectures until October 7th, and new epidemic prevention guidelines will be put into effect.
