Onsarigo-san empowered Nagai, a pacemaker in elementary school track and field, and three people broke the tournament record|The Yamagata Shimbun
The children running in the women's 800 metres.
On the left is pacemaker Joseph Orsarigo = Nagai City, Koyo Seiki Athlete Field Nagai
On the 26th, the Nagai City elementary school goodwill track and field tournament was held at the Koyo Seiki Athlete Field Nagai (City Athletics Stadium) in the city, and in some of the races, long-distance runner Joseph Onsaligo (27) = from Kenya, who belongs to Nasu Construction (the same city), served as a pacemaker.
We set a new record for the tournament of three people with a light run.
Onsaligo pulled the children in a total of four races in the men's 1000m and women's 800m.
In the men's category, Chiya Koseki (Hirano 6th graders) finished 3 minutes 4 seconds 38, and first-ranked runner-up Ransei Shikama (six-year-old Isazawa-no-ko six-year-old) scored 2 minutes 34 seconds 17, and Rin Asano (six-year-old Nagai-ko six-year-old) scored 2 minutes 35 seconds 73, each surpassing the tournament's record.
Koseki-san said, “It was hard to keep up with the fast Onsarigo players, but I was able to put up with it,” and Shikama said, “Onsarigo said, ‘Pace-up’.”
Onsarigo said, "The children's run was good. I was also able to do a perfect job."
This is the 39th time the tournament has been organized by the Nagai City Elementary School Sports Promotion Association, etc. to encourage sixth graders of elementary schools in the city to deepen their friendship.
